\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A symmetric pair keeps the backtracking 2-form}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{X=\{0,1\}} and let both ordered pairs be edges. Write
#{p=e_{01}e_{10}} and #{q=e_{10}e_{01}}. In the maximal prolongation
of \ref{fdip-00NW} both are nonzero. An undirected edge between the
same vertices has no #{2}-cell, so these two forms are surplus.}

\p{Let #{\Omega^2=k p\oplus k q}, set every product of total degree
#{3} or more to zero, and keep the bimodule actions of
\ref{fdip-00NT}: a path is multiplied on the left by the value at
its start and on the right by the value at its end. Set
##{d e_{01}=d e_{10}=p+q,\qquad dp=dq=0,}
and let #{d} on functions be the derivation of \ref{fdip-00NU}.
For a scalar value #{c\in k},
##{d(c\,e_{01})=c(p+q).}
The graded Leibniz expansion is
##{df\cdot e_{01}+f\cdot d e_{01}
=(f(0)-f(1))q+f(0)p+f(1)q
=f(0)(p+q),}
which agrees. The identity #{d(e_{01}f)=d(e_{01})\cdot f-e_{01}\,df}
is the same calculation with the value #{f(1)}. The generator
#{e_{10}} is symmetric. On a product of two #{1}-forms both sides
of graded Leibniz land in degree #{3} and vanish. Finally
##{d(df)=(f(1)-f(0))(p+q)+(f(0)-f(1))(p+q)=0.}
The class of #{p} is a basis element of this extension. The maximal
prolongation maps to every differential graded extension of the same
first-order calculus, so #{p} is already nonzero there. The same
holds for #{q}.}
